WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
the first signs of civil unrest since  the taliban returned to power in  afghanistan are emerging small  demonstrations have been reported in a  handful of cities witnesses say the  taliban shot dead three protesters who  were waving the national flag in  jalalabad meanwhile taliban fighters are  reported to have fired shots into the  air to disperse crowds trying to reach  kabul airport the islamist insurgents  have said repeatedly that they have  changed and want peace but many afghans  find that hard to believe  [Applause]  defiant scenes on the streets of  jalalabad  the taliban may say they have changed  [Music]  but their response to this show of  dissent tells a different story  dozens had gathered to lower the  militant group's banner and raise the  national flag when taliban fighters  opened fire  these distraught journalists say they  were beaten as they tried to cover the  protest  people with afghan flags and their hands  were chanting long live afghanistan and  saying the flag shouldn't be desecrated  we were taking pictures and videos as  they reached the roundabout  a taliban started hitting me with a gun  from behind as i was filming  shots also rang out today outside the  international airport in kabul  as the taliban sought to disperse crowds  attempting to flee  as foreign evacuation flights ramp up  there were unconfirmed reports of a  stampede at a gate at the airport  meanwhile the country's new rulers have  been meeting other political forces  taliban leader mullah abdul ghani barada  who arrived back from exile on tuesday  is expected to play a key role in  negotiations the taliban say they are  seeking to form an inclusive islamic  government  speaking from exile in the united arab  emirates ousted president ashraf ghani  back talks between the militant group  and top former government officials and  said he was in talks to return to  afghanistan  more now from journalists franz marty in  kabul franz what more can you tell us  about these anti-taliban protests  according to sources on the ground  protests were organized on social media  by civil society activists  who put out messages when to go on the  street and show support to the national  flag  i was told that uh beyond  supporting the national flag uh voicing  the national flag the protests didn't  had like a further agenda so they they  weren't like asking for a specific  political system or specific leaders um  then in jalalabad scenes turned ugly  with the taliban opening fire and also  like attacking journalists as was shown  in your report  in assad about and host all the cities  where similar protests were held there  have also been reports about gunfire but  their scenes have seemed to be less  dramatic however  figures close to the taliban  semi-official taliban  spokesperson had now announced that the  taliban are set to conduct a clearing  operation in hosts and have imposed the  indefinite curfew  which seems to be  a reaction to today's protests in host  which all shows that yeah the taliban  apparently don't like this and and are  not afraid to act decisively against it  we'll see what what developments occur  with that clearing operation taliban  co-founders you well know mullah  abdulgani bharata is one of a number of  group leaders who have returned from  exile tell us more about him and what  part he's likely to play  mullah baghdad  is of the old guard of the taliban he  was then  arrested spent years in a pakistani  prison  which apparently also impeded his health  he was then  released  in an effort to move forward  peace talks uh he was up on his release  appointed as the leader of the political  office  and presided over the negotiations that  led to the u.s taliban agreement signed  in february 2020 and subsequent  negotiations between the taliban and the  then afghan government  um his return  to uh afghanistan it is unclear what  exactly it means uh because there hasn't  been any official statement since he  returned however it might signal that  the taliban  decide of how and who to appoint at the  highest leadership the taliban have kind  of ousted the the former government from  afghanistan but so far they haven't  appointed um  their highest leader it is also  noteworthy that like uh main taliban  leader haibatullah and other deputies  their whereabouts are unknown and it is  unclear if and when they will surface  what's the latest from the airport franz  there are reports of the taliban  actually opening fire  the scenes at the airport are still  chaotic so the taliban have set up  checkpoints outside the airport inside  the airport parts of the airport are  secured by uh international forces and  uh then there have been hundreds and  thousands of people um  storming uh were like going to the  airport hoping to get out there have  been reports that taliban turned some  away beat some up  and uh given that there has been no  clear procedure  there have been huge crowd that  sometimes also caused stampedes  there have been reports about warning  shots uh that then might also have  injured people however with the  casualties it's very often hard to tell  whether this was due to the shooting or  just uh the crowd being out of control  and people being trampled  franz marty in kabul thank you so much  franz  for more we're joined by mark jacobson  he was an advisor to  former u.s secretary of defense ash  carter and also served as deputy nato  representative in afghanistan from 2009  to 2011.  the german government has admitted sir  operational errors in afghanistan  president biden continues to defend his  decision was the troop withdrawal a  mistake  well i've argued for months that the  troop withdrawal is a mistake the biden  administration set up a false choice  between  endless war and full withdrawal and  there were a lot of other choices so we  know that a small u.s uh and little  larger nato contingent a total of about  7 000 troops or so uh we now see what it  was holding together  we also know that one of the major  mistakes was  making very clear to the taliban and to  the afghan government that there would  be no more air support  and i'm not sure  any  uh well-trained military would be able  to operate  against its enemy without air support so  those were major mistakes there were  other things that could have been done a  longer-term training mission which is  not war  uh to help the afghan national security  forces fight  just briefly sir why do you think the  president did make that clear that there  would be no more air support was that to  joust the uh the the leadership in  afghanistan  uh it's an interesting thought but uh  you know what it really did was uh ended  up encouraging the taliban showing them  that there was a a true point beyond  which uh the afghan national security  forces would not not be able to  to fight um i think that the president  biden as the administration's been  saying is laser focused on withdrawal  and i do not think that they gave good  weight to the consequences in fact today  uh there have been reports that uh the  cia explained uh to the white house that  you would see this sort of collapse of  the afghan government i don't know the  timeline yet because i haven't read the  report but this is as we speak  in fact right now as we speak the  secretary of defense is briefing on this  uh very subject and what's going on in  afghanistan in his remarks on monday  biden said we gave them every chance to  determine their own future what we could  not provide them was the will to fight  for that future so essentially blaming  the country's leaders and security  forces for refusing to fight  do you agree with that assessment  absolutely not there are 60 000 afghan  national security forces who've been  killed fighting the taliban you still to  this day and i know there's not much  news coverage of it if any uh there are  still afghan units that are fighting the  taliban uh right now whether up in  tangier or in the south units that  refuse to surrender  you know if you think about the  situation the ansf were in and then  being told look we know the taliban are  in the middle of an offensive but we're  out of here and our air power's out of  here what choice did they really have  but to lay down their arms and maybe cut  a better deal  to try and get uh get their make sure  their families were safe i mean  the the withdrawal is what made the  collapse inevitable  what can international allies do or  maybe even have to do to stabilize the  situation in the country  well right now the most important thing  allies can do is get their foreign  nationals and the afghans who help  support the nato effort out  and i realize that the u.s really has to  bear the brunt of this effort uh given  the military heavy airlift capabilities  uh but we've seen you know it's it's  been really slow and problematic in  kabul and without getting into the  details there are some glimmers of hope  but if the plan is to get seven to nine  thousand uh just americans and afghans  uh and some other foreign nationals out  per day  um they're gonna have to do a lot better  um it's just been a couple of hundred uh  if that the last couple of days let's  hope today proves to be better but the  focus again has to be on getting people  out and then the international community  can work together to hold the taliban to  what i think are empty promises uh about  uh you know respect for human rights and  women's rights but if the taliban want  to go down that path and the  international community has to work  together to hold them to that promise  mark jacobson much appreciated sir  anytime thank you  a plane carrying evacuees from  afghanistan has arrived at frankfurt  airport the first of a number of special  lufthansa flights bringing german  citizens and afghans to germany military  planes took them from kabul to tashkent  in uzbekistan from there the lufthansa  flight brought them to frankfurt germany  has taken 500 people out of afghanistan  since the taliban took over here are  some of the first arrivals  it was really awful for my child it was  terrible for the three of us  people were shooting the soldiers were  shooting they wanted to prevent people  from storming the  is airport things were getting worse  every day  i didn't know how to help my family  everything is so bad  i could only save us  but i can't save the rest of my family  we pushed our way through  and then my little son fell down  and then an american showed kindness  and he realized we were at the end of  our tether he took our passports and  said i'm going to check if everything is  okay  i'm happy to be back and i thank the  german government for making it possible  for us to be here again

